# The issue
Some quantities are output at the previous/next timestep, rather than the current timestep. This depends on whether the GPU version of the code is used (note that it is the CPU version that actually exhibits the wrong behaviour).

An offset of a single timestep is probably not an issue for production runs (where the output interval is typically much larger than the timestep), but can cause a lot of confusion if one is trying to validate the GPU version by comparing its outputs to the CPU version.

The output below is generated by running `python check_output_times.py` after `pc_run`

# On CPU
Snapshots are output one timestep later than expected. For slices, the issue is
more serious, as slices which are taken from pencils are output at the correct
time but slices which are taken from the f-array are output with the values
from one timestep later (but still with the timestamp of the expected timestep!)
```
Time series:
        it:             [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        t:              [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialm:       [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Averages:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmz[:,0]:         [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmxy[:,0,0]:      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Snapshots:
        t:              [0.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0]
        special[0,0,0]: [0.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0]

Slices:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        xy.special[:,0,0]:      [1. 2. 3. 4. 5.]
        xy.specialpenc[:,0,0]:  [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Power spectra:
        t:              [1. 2. 3. 4. 5.]
        sqrt(sp[:,0]):  [1. 2. 3. 4. 5.]
```

# On GPU
(uncomment `GPU = gpu_astaroth` in src/Makefile.local)
All outputs are at the correct time. The only problem is that in the power
spectrum output, the first iteration has the wrong timestamp.
```
Time series:
        it:             [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        t:              [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialm:       [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Averages:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmz[:,0]:         [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        specialmxy[:,0,0]:      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Snapshots:
        t:              [0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]
        special[0,0,0]: [0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]

Slices:
        t:                      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        xy.special[:,0,0]:      [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        xy.specialpenc[:,0,0]:  [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]

Power spectra:
        t:              [1. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
        sqrt(sp[:,0]):  [0. 1. 2. 3. 4.]
```
